From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ilya Vysotsky <il.mohoff2016@yandex.ru>
Date: Thu, 24 Sep 2026 07:16:53 +0400
Subject: [PATCH] glsl-to-cxx: translate WebRender's fragment shaders to HLSL
 for feature level 9_3

The first step toward drawing pages on GPUs that stop at Direct3D feature
level 9_3, like the Adreno 3xx in the Lumia 550/650/950. WebRender cannot
run there: its vertex shaders read every primitive's data from textures,
and 9_3 has no vertex texture fetch. The plan is to keep SWGL's vertex
stage on the CPU, where it already runs, and give the per-pixel stage to
the GPU.

hlsl.rs translates a preprocessed fragment shader from the same HIR that
SWGL's C++ comes from, into ps_4_0_level_9_3. It emits only what main()
reaches. Varyings are packed into at most eight float4 texture
coordinates: interpolated ones first, then flat ones. Flat values that do
not fit are read from a float texture, one run of texels per primitive.
Where 9_3 lacks something, the output works around it:
- bitwise operations are emulated with arithmetic;
- loops without a constant bound are unrolled to a limit with a break;
- gl_FragCoord arrives as a varying, because SV_Position cannot be read;
- texelFetch samples at the texel centre, because SampleLevel is missing;
- dFdy is negated for a Y-flipped vertex stage;
- array results travel in structs.

Checked on the PC with fxc /T ps_4_0_level_9_3 against all 39 SWGL
variants: 35 compile. The four that do not (cs_blur x2, ps_quad_gradient,
cs_svg_filter_node) exceed the 512-instruction limit of ps_2_x; they stay
on the CPU for now. `glsl-to-cxx --hlsl <dir> <files>` reproduces the run.

Not used by the build yet.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
